                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>].
cdao:OBI_0200002 cdao:IAO_0000111 "logistic-log curve fitting"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Typically used in an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) to model the relationship between optical density (OD) and dilution. In this case a and d correspond to the theoretical OD of the assay at zero and infinite concentrations, respectively; c is the dilution associated with the point of symmetry of the sigmoid and is located at the midpoint of the assay found at the inflection point of the curve; b is a curvature parameter and is related to the slope of the curve."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A logistic-log curve fitting is a curve fitting where a curve of the form y=d+((a-d)/(1+(x/c)^b)) is obtained, where a, b, c, and d are determined so to optimize its fit to the input data points (x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n)."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Ryan Brinkman"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "ARTICLE: Plikaytis B.D. et al. (1991), J. Clin. Microbiol. 29(7): 1439-1448"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "logistic-log curve fitting"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200072,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200174 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200003 cdao:IAO_0000111 "logit-log curve fitting"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Typically used in an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) to model the relationship between optical density (OD) and dilution. In this case OD_0 (also referred to OD_min) and OD_infty (also referred to OD_max) correspond to the theoretical OD of the assay at zero and infinite concentrations, respectively."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A logit-log curve fitting is a curve fitting where first the limits y_0 an y_infty of y when x->0 and x->infinity, respectively, are estimated from the input data points (x_1, y_1), (x_2,y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n). Then a curve with equation log((y-y_0)/(y_infty-y))=a+b log(x) is obtained, where a and b are determined to optimize its fit to the input data points."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 "The above definition refers to the 'fully specified' logit-log model. The reduced form of this, when it is assumed that y_0=0, is named 'partially specified' logit-log model."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "ARTICLE: Plikaytis B.D. et al. (1991), J. Clin. Microbiol. 29(7): 1439-1448"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "logit-log curve fitting"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200174 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200072.
cdao:OBI_0200004 cdao:IAO_0000111 "log-log curve fitting"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Typically used in an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) to model the relationship between optical density (OD) and dilution."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A log-log curve fitting is a curve fitting where first a logarithmic transformation is applied both to the x and the y coordinates of the input data points (x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n), and then coefficients a and b are determined to optimize the fit of log(y)=a+b*log(x) to these input data points."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "ARTICLE: Plikaytis B.D. et al. (1991), J. Clin. Microbiol. 29(7): 1439-1446"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "log-log curve fitting"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200174 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200072.
cdao:OBI_0200005 cdao:IAO_0000111 "feature extraction objective"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A feature extraction objective is a data transformation objective where the aim of the data transformation is to generate quantified values from a scanned image."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "TERM: http://mged.sourceforge.net/ontologies/MGEDOntology.owl#feature_extraction"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "feature extraction objective"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200166.
cdao:OBI_0200006 cdao:IAO_0000111 "biexponential transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This type of transformation is typically used in flow cytometry."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A biexponential transformation is a data transformation that, for each (one dimensional) real number input x, outputs an approximation (found, e.g. with the Newton's method) to a solution y of the equation B(y)-x=0, where B denotes a b transformation."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Joseph Spliden"@en,
                                  "Ryan Brinkman"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://flowcyt.sourceforge.net/gating/latest.pdf"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "biexponential transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200072,
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000451 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200174 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200007 cdao:IAO_0000111 "box-cox transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A box-cox transformation is a data transformation according to the methods of Box and Cox as described in the article Box, G. E. P. and Cox, D.R. (1964) An analysis of transformations. Journal of Royal Statistical Society, Series B, vol. 26, pp. 211-246."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Ryan Brinkman"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 """ARTICLE: Box, G. E. P. and Cox, D.R. (1964), \"An analysis of transformations\", Journal of Royal Statistical Society, Series B, vol. 26, pp. 211-246."""@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "box-cox transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169.
cdao:OBI_0200008 cdao:IAO_0000111 "hyperlog transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This type of transformation is typically used in flow cytometry"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A hyperlog transformation ia a data transformation that, for each (one dimensional) real number input x, outputs an approximation (found, e.g. with the Newton's method) to a solution y of the equation EH(y)-x=0, where EH denotes an eh transformation."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Joseph Spliden"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 """ARTICLE: Bagwell C.B. (2006), \"Hyperlog - a flexible log-like  transform for negative, zero, and positive valued data\", Cytometry A 64, 34-42.\""""@en,
                                  "http://flowcyt.sourceforge.net/gating/latest.pdf"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "hyperlog transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169.
cdao:OBI_0200010 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess scale group transformation one-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Loess scale group normalization applied to data from two one-channel expression microarray assays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess scale group transformation one-channel is a loess scale group transformation consisting in the application of a scale adjustment following a loess group transformation one-channel, to render the M group variances similar."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's adjustment based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess scale group transformation one-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200025.
cdao:OBI_0200011 cdao:IAO_0000111 "logical transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This type of transformation is typically used in flow cytometry."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A logical transformation is a data transformation that, for each (one dimensional) real number input x, outputs an approximation (found, e.g. with the Newton's method) to a solution y of the equation S(y)-x=0, where S denotes an s transformation."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Joseph Spliden"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://flowcyt.sourceforge.net/gating/latest.pdf"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "logical transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200012 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess scale group transformation two-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Loess scale group normalization applied to data from a two-channel expression microarray assay."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess scale group transformation two-channel is a loess scale group transformation consisting in the application of a scale adjustment following a loess group transformation two-channel, to render the M group variances similar."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Adjusted from MGED Ontology"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess scale group transformation two-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200025.
cdao:OBI_0200013 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess global transformation one-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Loess global normalization applied to data from two one-channel expression microarray assays, where the curve is obtained using all reporters. The goal is to remove intensity-dependent biases."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess global transformation one-channel is a loess global transformation in the special case where the input is the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from two related one-channel assays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess global transformation one-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200023.
cdao:OBI_0200014 cdao:IAO_0000111 "split-scale transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This type of transformation is typically used in flow cytometry"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A split-scale transformation is a data transformation which is an application of a function f described as follows to a (one dimensional) real number input. f(x)=a*x+b if x=for x>t; where log denotes a logarithmic transformation and a, b, c, d, r, t are real constants, with a, c, d, r, t positive, chosen so that f is continuous with a continuous derivative at the transition point t."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Joseph Spliden"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://flowcyt.sourceforge.net/gating/latest.pdf"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "split-scale transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200000.
cdao:OBI_0200015 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess global transformation two-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Loess global normalization applied to data from a two-channel expression microarray assay, where the curve is obtained using all reporters. The goal is to remove intensity-dependent biases."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess global transformation two-channel is a loess global transformation in the special case where the input the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from the two channels of a two-channel assay."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess global transformation two-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200023.
cdao:OBI_0200016 cdao:IAO_0000111 "sine transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "sine(0)=0, sine(pi/2)=1, sine(pi)=0, sine(3*pi/2)=-1, sine(pi/6)=1/2, sine(x+2*k*pi)=sine(x) where k is any integer, etc."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A sine transformation is a data transformation which consists in applying the sine function to a (one dimensional) real number input. The sine function is one of the basic trigonometric functions and a definition is provided, e.g., at http://mathworld.wolfram.com/Sine.html."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://mathworld.wolfram.com/Sine.html"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "sine transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200017 cdao:IAO_0000111 "cosine transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "cosine(0)=1, cosine(pi/2)=0, cosine(pi)=-1, cosine(3*pi/2)=0, cosine(pi/3)=1/2, cosine(x+2*k*pi)=cosine(x) where k is any integer, etc."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A cosine transformation is a data transformation which consists in applying the cosine function to a (one dimensional) real number input. The cosine function is one of the basic trigonometric functions and a definition is provided, e.g., at http://mathworld.wolfram.com/Cosine.html."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://mathworld.wolfram.com/Cosine.html"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "cosine transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169.
cdao:OBI_0200018 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess group transformation one-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "A loess group transformation one-channel is a loess group transformation in the special case where the input is the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from two related one-channel assays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess group transformation one-channel is a loess group transformation in the special case where the input is the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from two related one-channel assays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess group transformation one-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200024.
cdao:OBI_0200019 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess group transformation two-channel"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "A loess group transformation two-channel is a loess group transformation in the special case where the input is the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from the two channels of a two-channel assay."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess group transformation two-channel is a loess group transformation in the special case where the input is the result of an MA transformation applied to intensities from the two channels of a two-channel assay."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess group transformation two-channel"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200024.
cdao:OBI_0200020 cdao:IAO_0000111 "homogeneous polynomial transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "a*x, with a non-zero, is a homogeneous polynomial of degree 1 in 1 variable, a*x^2, with a non-zero, is a homogeneous polynomial of degree 2 in 1 variable; a_1*x_1+...+a_n*x_n, with at least one of the a_i's non-zero, is a homogeneous polynomial of degree one in n variables; a*x_n^3+b*x_1*x_2*x_3, with at least one of a and b non-zero, is a homogeneous polynomial of degree 3 in n variables."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A homogeneous polynomial transformation is a polynomial transformation where all the term of the polynomial have the same degree."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://mathworld.wolfram.com/HomogeneousPolynomial.html"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "homogeneous polynomial transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200093.
cdao:OBI_0200021 cdao:IAO_0000111 "linlog transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This can be used for microarray normalization, e.g. to normalize the data from a two-channel expression microarray assay, as described in PMID 16646782."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A linlog transformation is a data transformation, described in PMID 16646782, whose input is a matrix [y_ik] and whose output is a matrix obtained by applying formula (9) of this paper, where values below an appropriately determined threshold (dependent on the row i) are transformed via a polynomial of degree 1, and values above this threshold are transformed via a logarithm."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PMID: 16646782"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "linlog transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200022 cdao:IAO_0000111 "variance stabilizing transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 """This can be used for expression microarray assay normalization and it is referred to as \"variance stabilizing normalization\", according to the procedure described e.g. in PMID 12169536."""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A variance stabilizing transformation is a data transformation, described in PMID 12169536, whose input is a matrix [y_ik] and whose output is a matrix obtained by applying formula (6) in this paper. One of the goals is to obtain an output matrix whose rows have equal variances. The method relies on various assumptions described in the paper."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "variance stabilising transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PMID: 12169536"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "variance stabilizing transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169.
cdao:OBI_0200023 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess global transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess global transformation is a loess transformation where only one loess fitting is performed, utilizing one subset of (or possibly all of) the data points in the input so that there is only one resulting loess curve y=f(x) which is used for the transformation."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess global transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200071.
cdao:OBI_0200024 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess group transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess group transformation is a loess transformation where the input is partitioned into groups and for each group a loess fitting is performed, utilizing a subset of (or possibly all of) the data points in that group. Thus, a collection of loess curves y=f_i(x) is generated, one per group. Each (x, y) in the input is transformed into (x, y-f_i(x)), where f_i(x) is the curve corresponding to the group to which that data point belongs."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess group transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200071.
cdao:OBI_0200025 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess scale group transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess scale group transformation is a data transformation consisting in the application of a scale adjustment following a loess group transformation, to render the group variances for the second variable (y) similar.  Has objective scaling."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess scale group transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200183 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200024 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:RO_0002087 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200185.
cdao:OBI_0200026 cdao:IAO_0000111 "total intensity transformation single"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This can be used as a simple normalization method for expression microarray assays. For example, each intensity from a one-channel microarray assay is multiplied by a constant so that the output mean intensity over the microarray equals a desired target T (the multiplicative constant in this case is the T/(mean intensity))."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A total intensity transformation single is a data transformation that takes as input an n-dimensional (real) vector and multiplies each component of this vector by a coefficient, where the coefficient is obtained by taking the sum of the input components or of a subset of these, multiplied by a constant of choice."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Helen Parkinson"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Adjusted from MGED Ontology"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000232 """Note that if the word \"sum\" is replaced by the word \"mean\" in the definition, the resulting definition is equivalent."""@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "total intensity transformation single"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200027 cdao:IAO_0000111 "total intensity transformation paired"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This can be used as a simple normalization method for the two channels from a two-channel expression microarray assay or from two related one-channel expression microarray assays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A total intensity transformation paired is a data transformation that takes as input two n-dimensional (real) vectors and multiplies each component of the first vector by a coefficient, where the coefficient is obtained by taking the ratio of the sum of the second input components or of a subset of these by the sum of the first input components or of a subset of these (the same subset is used for the two vectors)."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Adjusted from MGED Ontology"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000232 """Note that if the word \"sum\" is replaced by the word \"mean\" in the definition, the resulting definition is equivalent."""@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "total intensity transformation paired"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200028 cdao:IAO_0000111 "quantile transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 """This can be used for expression microarray assay normalization and it is referred to as \"quantile normalization\", according to the procedure described e.g. in PMID 12538238."""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A quantile transformation is a data transformation that takes as input a collection of data sets, where each can be thought as an n-dimensional (real) vector, and which transforms each data set so that the resulting output data sets have equal quantiles."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "quantile transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000674 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200177 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200181.
cdao:OBI_0200029 cdao:IAO_0000111 "mean centering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This can be used as a normalization method in expression microarray assays. For example, given a two-channel microarray assay, the log ratios of the two channels (M values) can be mean-centered."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A mean centering is a data transformation that takes as input an n-dimensional (real) vector, performs a mean calculation on its components, and subtracts the resulting mean from each component of the input."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "mean centring"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "mean centering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200169.
cdao:OBI_0200030 cdao:IAO_0000111 "median centering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "This can be used as a normalization method in expression microarray assays. For example, given a two-channel microarray assay, the log ratios of the two channels (M values) can be median-centered."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A median centering is a data transformation that takes as input an n-dimensional (real) vector, performs a median calculation on its components, and subtracts the resulting median from each component of the input."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "median centring"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "median centering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200031 cdao:IAO_0000111 "differential expression analysis objective"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Analyses implemented by the SAM (http://www-stat.stanford.edu/~tibs/SAM), PaGE (www.cbil.upenn.edu/PaGE) or GSEA (www.broad.mit.edu/gsea/) algorithms and software"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A differential expression analysis objective is a data transformation objective whose input consists of expression levels of entities (such as transcripts or proteins), or of sets of such expression levels, under two or more conditions and whose output reflects which of these are likely to have different expression across such conditions."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "differential expression analysis objective"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200166.
cdao:OBI_0200032 cdao:IAO_0000111 "K-fold cross validation method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "K-fold cross-validation randomly partitions the original sample into K subsamples. Of the K subsamples, a single subsample is retained as the validation data for testing the model, and the remaining K - 1 subsamples are used as training data. The cross-validation process is then repeated K times (the folds), with each of the K subsamples used exactly once as the validation data. The K results from the folds then can be averaged (or otherwise combined) to produce a single estimation. The advantage of this method over repeated random sub-sampling is that all observations are used for both training and validation, and each observation is used for validation exactly once. 10-fold cross-validation is commonly used";
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Person:Helen Parkinson";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "Tina Boussard";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "K-fold cross validation method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200188 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0000792.
cdao:OBI_0200033 cdao:IAO_0000111 "leave one out cross validation method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "The authors conducted  leave-one-out cross validation to estimate the strength and accuracy of the differentially expressed filtered genes. http://bioinformatics.oxfordjournals.org/cgi/content/abstract/19/3/368";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "is a data transformation :  leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) involves using a single observation from the original sample as the validation data, and the remaining observations as the training data. This is repeated such that each observation in the sample is used once as the validation data";
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 "2009-11-10. Tracker: https://sourceforge.net/tracker/?func=detail&aid=2893049&group_id=177891&atid=886178";
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Person:Helen Parkinson";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "leave one out cross validation method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0000792,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200188 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200034 cdao:IAO_0000111 "jackknifing method";
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "simple weighting procedure is suggested for combining information over alleles and loci, and sample variances may be estimated by a jackknife procedure";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Jacknifing is a re-sampling data transformation process used to estimate the precision of sampling statistics and is a resampling method";
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson";
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "jackknifing"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resampling_%28statistics%29";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "jackknifing method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0000792,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200188 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200035 cdao:IAO_0000111 "boostrapping"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Although widely accepted that high throughput biological data are typically highly noisy, the effects that this uncertainty has upon the conclusions we draw from these data are often overlooked. However, in order to assign any degree of confidence to our conclusions, we must quantify these effects. Bootstrap resampling is one method by which this may be achieved.";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Bootstrapping is a statistical method for estimating the sampling distribution of a statistic by sampling with replacement from the original data, most often with the purpose of deriving robust estimates of standard errors and confidence intervals of a population parameter like a mean, median, proportion, odds ratio, correlation coefficient or regression coefficient"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "Bootstrapping is a data transformation process which estimates the precision of sampling statistics by drawing randomly with replacement from a set of data points";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "boostrapping"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0000792,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200188 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200036 cdao:IAO_0000111 "Benjamini and Hochberg false discovery rate correction method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Statistical significance of the 8 most represented biological processes (GO level 4) among E7 6 month upregulated genes following analysis with DAVID software; Benjamini-Hochberg FDR (false discovery rate)";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A data transformation process in which the Benjamini and Hochberg method sequential p-value procedure  is applied with the aim of correcting false discovery rate";
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 """2011-03-31: [PRS]. 
specified input and output of dt which were missing"""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson",
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "Helen Parkinson";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "Benjamini and Hochberg false discovery rate correction method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000175 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000293 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0001442 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000791 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200163,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000662 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200037 cdao:IAO_0000111 "pareto scaling"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A pareto scaling is a data transformation that divides all measurements of a variable by the square root of the standard deviation of that variable."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PMID:16762068"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "pareto scaling"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200040 cdao:IAO_0000111 "modular decomposition"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Molecular decomposition is the partition of a network into distinct subgraphs for the purpose of identifying functional clusters. The network data is run through any of several existing algorithms designed to partition a network into distinct subgraphs for the purpose of isolating groups of functionally linked biological elements such as proteins."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "editor"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "modular decomposition"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200080.
cdao:OBI_0200041 cdao:IAO_0000111 "k-means clustering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A k-means clustering is a data transformation which achieves a class discovery or partitioning objective, which takes as input a collection of objects (represented as points in multidimensional space) and which partitions them into a specified number k of clusters. The algorithm attempts to find the centers of natural clusters in the data. The most common form of the algorithm starts by partitioning the input points into k initial sets, either at random or using some heuristic data. It then calculates the mean point, or centroid, of each set. It constructs a new partition by associating each point with the closest centroid. Then the centroids are recalculated for the new clusters, and the algorithm repeated by alternate applications of these two steps until convergence, which is obtained when the points no longer switch clusters (or alternatively centroids are no longer changed)."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi",
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-means"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "k-means clustering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200171,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000648 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200178 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200172 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200175.
cdao:OBI_0200042 cdao:IAO_0000111 "hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A hierarchical clustering is a data transformation which achieves a class discovery objective, which takes as input data item and builds a hierarchy of clusters. The traditional representation of this hierarchy is a tree (visualized by a dendrogram), with the individual input objects at one end (leaves) and a single cluster containing every object at the other (root)."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_clustering#Hierarchical_clustering"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000648 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200178 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200175.
cdao:OBI_0200043 cdao:IAO_0000111 "average linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "An average linkage hierarchical clustering is an agglomerative hierarchical clustering which generates successive clusters based on a distance measure, where the distance between two clusters is calculated as the average distance between objects from the first cluster and objects from the second cluster."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "average linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200154.
cdao:OBI_0200044 cdao:IAO_0000111 "complete linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "an agglomerative hierarchical clustering which generates successive clusters based on a distance measure, where the distance between two clusters is calculated as the maximum distance between objects from the first cluster and objects from the second cluster."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "complete linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200154.
cdao:OBI_0200045 cdao:IAO_0000111 "single linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A single linkage hierarchical clustering is an agglomerative hierarchical clustering which generates successive clusters based on a distance measure, where the distance between two clusters is calculated as the minimum distance between objects from the first cluster and objects from the second cluster."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "single linkage hierarchical clustering"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200154.
cdao:OBI_0200049 cdao:IAO_0000111 "Benjamini and Yekutieli false discovery rate correction method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "The expression set was compared univariately between the stroke patients and controls, gene list was generated using False Discovery Rate correction (Benjamini and Yekutieli)";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A data transformation in which the Benjamini and Yekutieli method is applied with the aim of correcting false discovery rate";
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 """2011-03-31: [PRS]. 
specified input and output of dt which were missing"""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra"@en,
                                  "Helen Parkinson";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "Helen Parkinson";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "Benjamini and Yekutieli false discovery rate correction method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200163,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000175 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000293 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0001442 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000791 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000662 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200050 cdao:IAO_0000111 "dimensionality reduction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A dimensionality reduction is data partitioning which transforms each input m-dimensional vector (x_1, x_2, ..., x_m) into an output n-dimensional vector (y_1, y_2, ..., y_n), where n is smaller than m."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "data projection";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "dimensionality reduction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000648 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200178 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200175.
cdao:OBI_0200051 cdao:IAO_0000111 "principal components analysis dimensionality reduction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A principal components analysis dimensionality reduction is a dimensionality reduction achieved by applying principal components analysis and by keeping low-order principal components and excluding higher-order ones."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "pca data reduction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "principal components analysis dimensionality reduction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200050.
cdao:OBI_0200052 cdao:IAO_0000111 "probabilistic algorithm"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A probabilistic algorithm is one which involves an element of probability or randomness in the transformation of the data."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "probabilistic algorithm"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200000.
cdao:OBI_0200053 cdao:IAO_0000111 "expectation maximization"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "EM is a probabilistic algorithm used to estimate the maximum likelihood of parameters from existing data where the model involves unobserved latent variables. The input to this method is the data model for which the estimation is performed over and the output is an approximated probability function."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "expectation maximization"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200052.
cdao:OBI_0200054 cdao:IAO_0000111 "global modularity calculation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A network graph quality calculation in which an input data set of subgraph modules and their in-degree and out-degree qualities is used to calculate the average modularity of subgraphs within the network."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Richard Scheuermann"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Richard Scheuermann"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "global modularity calculation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200147.
cdao:OBI_0200055 cdao:IAO_0000111 "dye swap merge"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A dye swap merge is a replicate analysis which takes as input data from paired two-channel microarray assays where the sample labeled with one dye in the first assay is labeled with the other dye in the second assay and vice versa. The output for each reporter is obtained by combining its (raw or possibly pre-processed) M values in the two assays, where the M value in an assay is defined as the difference of the log intensities in the two channels. This can be used as a normalization step, when appropriate assumptions are met."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: James Malone"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "dye swap merge"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200057.
cdao:OBI_0200056 cdao:IAO_0000111 "moving average"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "The moving average is often used to handle data from tiling arrays."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A moving average is a data transformation in which center calculations, usually mean calculations, are performed on values within a sliding window across the input data set."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "moving average"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000679 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                  [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000425 ; 
                                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200170.
cdao:OBI_0200057 cdao:IAO_0000111 "replicate analysis"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "Replicate analysis can be used in microarray analysis to identify and potentially exclude low quality data."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A replicate analysis is a data transformation in which data from replicates are combined, e.g. through descriptive statistics calculations, and the results might be utilized for a variety of purposes, like assessing reproducibility, identifying outliers, normalizing, etc."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON:Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "replicate analysis"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200058 cdao:IAO_0000111 "b cell epitope prediction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A B cell epitope prediction takes as input an antigen sequence, and through an analysis of this sequence, produces as output a prediction of the likelihood the biomaterial is a B Cell Epitope."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "b cell epitope prediction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200081 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200187.
cdao:OBI_0200059 cdao:IAO_0000111 "mhc binding prediction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "An MHC binding prediction takes an input of a biomaterial sequence and through an analysis of this sequence, produces as output a prediction of the likelihood that the biomaterial will bind to an MHC molecule."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "mhc binding prediction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200081 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200187.
cdao:OBI_0200060 cdao:IAO_0000111 "t cell epitope prediction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A T cell epitope prediction takes as input an antigen sequence, and through an analysis of this sequence, produces as output a prediction of the likelihood the biomaterial is a T cell epitope."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Helen Parkinson"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "t cell epitope prediction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200081 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200187.
cdao:OBI_0200061 cdao:IAO_0000111 "data imputation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Imputation is a means of filling in missing data values from a predictive distribution of the missing values.  The predictive distribution can be created either based on a formal statistical model (i,e, a multivariate normal distribution) or an algorithm."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Monnie McGee"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "ARTICLE: Little, RJA and Rubin, DB (2002).  Statistical Analysis with Missing Data, Second Edition.  John Wiley: Hoboken New Jersey, pp. 59-60."@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "data imputation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200000.
cdao:OBI_0200062 cdao:IAO_0000111 "continuum mass spectrum"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A continuum mass spectrum is a data transformation that contains the full profile of the detected signals for a given ion."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "PERSON: Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Tina Boussard"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "continuum mass spectrum"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200197 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200085.
cdao:OBI_0200063 cdao:IAO_0000111 "characteristic path length calculation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Quantifying subgraph navigability based on shortest-path length averaged over all pairs of subgraph vertices"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "PERSON: Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "characteristic path length calculation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200197 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200085.
cdao:OBI_0200064 cdao:IAO_0000111 "centroid mass spectrum"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A centroid mass spectrum is a data transformation in which many points are used to delineate a mass spectral peak, is converted into mass-centroided data by a data compression algorithm. The centroided mass peak is located at the weighted center of mass of the profile peak. The normalized area of the peak provides the mass intensity data."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Person:Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "centroid mass spectrum calculation",
                                  "centroiding";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "centroid mass spectrum"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "centroid mass spectrum"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200085,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200197 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200066 cdao:IAO_0000111 "Holm-Bonferroni family-wise error rate correction method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "t-tests were used with the type I error adjusted for multiple comparisons, Holm's correction (HOLM 1979), and false discovery rate, http://www.genetics.org/cgi/content/full/172/2/1179";
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "a data transformation that performs more than one hypothesis test simultaneously, a closed-test procedure, that  controls the familywise error rate for all the k hypotheses at level α in the strong sense. Objective: multiple testing correction";
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 """2011-03-14: [PRS]. Class Label has been changed to address the conflict with the definition
Also added restriction to specify the output to be a FWER adjusted p-value

The 'editor preferred term' should be removed"""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra"@en,
                                  "Person:Helen Parkinson";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holm%E2%80%93Bonferroni_method";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "Holm-Bonferroni family-wise error rate correction method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000791 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000175 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000293 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200073,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0001265 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200070 cdao:IAO_0000111 "edge weighting"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000123;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Edge weighting is the substitution or transformation of edge length using numerical data.  Data input include a symmetric adjacency matrix for a network and a second data set, for example a list of interactor pairs and a confidence score associated with the experimental detection of each pair's interaction.  Each element in the adjacency matrix is transformed or replaced with the corresponding number in the second data set.  Output data are a modified adjacency matrix reflecting the transformed state of the network."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Tina Hernandez-Boussard"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "editor"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000232 """was classified under algorithm class which is not acceptable super-class
TO BE DEALT WITH STILL BY RICHARD.  JAMES"""@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "edge weighting"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200080.
cdao:OBI_0200071 cdao:IAO_0000111 "loess transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A loess transformation is a data transformation that takes as input a collection of real number pairs (x, y) and, after performing (one or more) loess fittings, utilizes the resulting curves to transform each (x, y) in the input into (x, y-f(x)) where f(x) is one of the fitted curves."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra";
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "OTHER: Editor's generalization based on MGED Ontology term"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "loess transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200169,
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200167 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200072 cdao:IAO_0000111 "curve fitting data transformation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A curve fitting is a data transformation that has objective curve fitting and that consists of finding a curve which matches a series of data points and possibly other constraints."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curve_fitting"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "curve fitting data transformation"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200000;
                 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> ([<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000656 ; 
                                                                                                           <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                                                           a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]
                                                                                                           [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200174 ; 
                                                                                                           <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                                                                                           a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>]) ; 
                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>].
cdao:OBI_0200073 cdao:IAO_0000111 "family wise error rate correction method"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A family wise error rate correction method is a multiple testing procedure that controls the probability of at least one false positive.";
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 """2011-03-31: [PRS]. 
creating a defined class by specifying the necessary output of dt
allows correct classification of FWER dt"""@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Philippe Rocca-Serra"@en,
                                  "Monnie McGee";
                 cdao:IAO_0000118 "FWER correction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "Dudoit, Sandrine and van der Laan, Mark J. (2008) Multiple Testing Procedures with Applications to Genomics.  New York: Springer , p. 19";
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "family wise error rate correction method"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0000791 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200089;
                 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0001265 ; 
                                                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000299 ; 
                                                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>].
cdao:OBI_0200074 cdao:IAO_0000111 "submatrix extraction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "When presented with the data from an expression microarray experiment in the form of a matrix, whose rows correspond to genes and whose columns correspond to samples, if one filters some of the genes and/or some of the samples out, the resulting data set corresponds to a submatrix of the original set."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A submatrix extraction is a projection whose input is a matrix and whose output is a matrix obtained by selecting certain rows and columns from the input. (Note that, if one represents the input matrix as a vector obtained by concatenating its rows, then extracting a submatrix is equivalent to projecting this vector into that composed by the entries belonging to the rows and columns of interest from the input matrix.)"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000116 "Note that this can be considered as a special case of projection if one represents the input matrix as a vector obtained by concatenating its rows. Then extracting a submatrix is equivalent to projecting this vector into the entries belonging to the rows and columns of interest from the input matrix."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "WEB: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submatrix"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "submatrix extraction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200050.
cdao:OBI_0200075 cdao:IAO_0000111 "row submatrix extraction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "When presented with the data from an expression microarray experiment in the form of a matrix, whose rows correspond to genes and whose columns correspond to samples, if one filters some of the genes out, the resulting data set corresponds to a row submatrix of the original set."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A row submatrix extraction is a submatrix extraction where all the columns of the input matrix are retained and selection only occurs on the rows."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "row submatrix extraction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200074.
cdao:OBI_0200076 cdao:IAO_0000111 "column submatrix extraction"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000112 "When presented with the data from an expression microarray experiment in the form of a matrix, whose rows correspond to genes and whose columns correspond to samples, if one filters some of the samples out, the resulting data set corresponds to a column submatrix of the original set."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A column submatrix extraction is a submatrix extraction where all the rows of the input matrix are retained and selection only occurs on the columns."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "column submatrix extraction"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200074.
cdao:OBI_0200077 cdao:IAO_0000111 "gating"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000125;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "Gating is a property-based vector selection with the objective of partitioning a data vector set into vector subsets based on dimension values of individual vectors (events), in which vectors represent individual physical particles (often cells) of a sample and dimension values represent light intensity qualities as measured by flow cytometry."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Josef Spidlen"@en,
                                  "Richard Scheuermann"@en,
                                  "Ryan Brinkman"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Richard Scheuermann"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Josef Spidlen"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en,
                                  "PERSON:Melanie Courtot"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Ryan Brinkman"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "gating"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200172 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 [<http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> cdao:OBI_0200157 ; 
                                  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> cdao:OBI_0000417 ; 
                                  a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction>],
                                 cdao:OBI_0200182,
                                 cdao:OBI_0200171.
cdao:OBI_0200078 cdao:IAO_0000111 "descriptive statistical calculation objective"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000122;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "A descriptive statistical calculation objective is a data transformation objective which concerns any calculation intended to describe a feature of a data set, for example, its center or its variability."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "Monnie McGee"@en,
                                  "James Malone"@en,
                                  "Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: Elisabetta Manduchi"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Monnie McGee"@en,
                                  "PERSON: James Malone"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Melanie Courtot"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "descriptive statistical calculation objective"@en;
                 rdfs:subClassOf cdao:OBI_0200166.
cdao:OBI_0200079 cdao:IAO_0000111 "arithmetic mean calculation"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000114 cdao:IAO_0000120;
                 cdao:IAO_0000115 "An arithmetic mean calculation is a descriptive statistics calculation in which the mean is calculated by taking the sum of all of the observations in a data set divided by the total number of observations. It gives a measure of the 'center of gravity' for the data set. It is also known as the first moment."@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000117 "Monnie McGee"@en,
                                  "Philippe Rocca-Serra",
                                  "James Malone"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000119 "PERSON: James Malone"@en,
                                  "PERSON: Monnie McGee"@en;
                 cdao:IAO_0000232 "From Monnie's file comments - need to add moment_calculation and center_calculation roles but they don't exist yet - (editor note added by James Jan 2008)"@en;
                 a <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class>;
                 rdfs:label "arithmetic mean calculation"@en;
